<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Montpellier</h1><p class="st-meta">985 CE – 1349 CE (approx.) · capital Montpellier</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The merchant town of Languedoc whose Guilhem lords founded a dynasty, whose university taught medicine, and where King James I of Aragon was born.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 985 the count of Melgueil granted lands at Montpellier to a man named Guilhem, whose descendants, all named William, ruled the growing town as lords. Montpellier rose quickly in the 11th and 12th centuries as a market and staging post on the road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The lords governed with an active merchant community and a strong consulate. Montpellier traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, cloth and dyes across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and its medical school, recognised as a faculty in 1220, became one of the most famous in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In 1204 the heiress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-of-montpellier">Marie of Montpellier</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-ii-of-aragon">Peter II of Aragon</a>; their son James I was born there in 1208.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The lordship passed to the Aragonese crown and then to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-majorca">Kingdom of Majorca</a>. In 1349 the hard-pressed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-iii-of-majorca">James III of Majorca</a> sold Montpellier to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-vi-of-france">Philip VI</a>, and it joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 985 CE – 1349 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">France and the Low Countries</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:79.30%;width:7.24%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>985 CE – 1349 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Montpellier</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>William VIII of Montpellier, Marie of Montpellier, James I of Aragon</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Occitan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Montpellier</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1167&amp;focus=lordship-of-montpellier" aria-label="Lordship of Montpellier on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4773 632 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Montpellier lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5093 888L4984 945M5093 888L5065 1017M5093 888L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4984 945h0M5065 1017h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5093 888h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5093 888h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Montpellier. <a href="/atlas?year=1167&amp;focus=lordship-of-montpellier">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
